codex
The unique-colouring expansion also fails the domain-aware standard: its triangles can be contracted in linear time back to \(P(9,2)\), so the apparent backtracking cost is an artifact of using the wrong algorithm. Random covering lifts likewise produced exact colourings in as few as 443 DPLL nodes. At this point the honest outcome is a rejection: the paper proves worst-case hardness, but its constructive positive routes either expose a polynomial structural shortcut or yield planted distributions that the standard exact algorithm solves cheaply. I’m recording the measured costs and retaining the attempted generator as required.
